etry/ listing. Confusions: The README gives the install plus init plus run as one chained && line in the intro, and again as three separate lines under 'Gate your own agent'. I took the three separate lines as the sequence, but it took a moment to be sure they are the same thing. | The README's init example does not include --task, but my first `arci init` failed with a KeyError on 'order_id' because the toolset needs a task. The fix (--task '{\"order_id\": \"order-7\"}') is mentioned only in prose after the code block ('--task sets the task') and in the hand-written descriptor example. The error message does say 'check the toolset factory and --task', which pointed me to it, but a reader whose toolset needs task fields hits a failed first command. | The README's sample demo wrapper hint (`${PIPESTATUS[0]}`) is bash-only; in this zsh shell it printed an empty exit code, so I had to rerun the 75-second demo under bash to get exit 0. This is my wrapper, not the README's fault, but worth knowing for facilitators. | The README is very long (about 35 KB) with a lot of evidence, history and statistics before 'Gate your own agent'; the own-agent path is found only after the demo, results, verdict table and how-it-works sections. I read all of it before acting. | The README says the scaffold writes one clean condition with no fault, and also warns that declaring a fault does not guarantee injection. My run therefore tested no faults at all, so the INCONCLUSIVE (8/8 vs 8/8) says little about the retry behaviour implied by the name 'checkout-retry'. I did not add --fault tool_timeout:reserve because the task did not ask for it. | I did not read docs/DESCRIPTOR.md or other docs; I did not need them. I did not understand how to choose the confirmatory N beyond 'use arci plan'; I did not run arci plan. | The recipe directory name suggests a README exists in the project copy (README.md was present in the copy); I deliberately did not open it to stay cold.. Reader notes: Machine s